<div><span style="white-space: pre; white-space: normal;"><font color="#0f172a">	</font></span></div><div><span style="white-space: pre; white-space: normal;">	</span></div><div><br></div><div><div><span style="white-space: pre; white-space: normal;">	</span></div><div>&nbsp;Conscious change on 03/14 night</div><div>主要病史</div><div>&nbsp;<span style="white-space:pre">	</span></div><div>&nbsp;The patient was brought to the emergency department after a sudden collapse at home. According to family members, the patient had been in usual health until shortly before the event when he/she suddenly complained of a severe headache described as the “worst headache of life.” This was followed by abrupt loss of consciousness within minutes. No preceding trauma was reported.</div><div>Emergency medical services were activated immediately. During transport, the patient remained unresponsive. No seizure activity was clearly witnessed, though the family reported brief generalized body stiffening at the onset of collapse. There was no known history of recent fever, infection, or head injury.</div><div>Upon arrival to the emergency department, the patient was comatose with a Glasgow Coma Scale of E1M4V1 (total score 6). Initial vital signs showed BP 115/67 mmHg, pulse 100 bpm, respiratory rate 18 breaths/min, temperature 35.7°C, and oxygen saturation 99% on supplemental oxygen. Neurological examination demonstrated bilaterally small pupils measuring 1.0 mm with sluggish light reflexes and withdrawal to painful stimuli.</div><div>An emergent non-contrast head CT demonstrated diffuse subarachnoid hemorrhage involving the basal cisterns and sylvian fissures, highly suspicious for ruptured intracranial aneurysm. The CECT was performed and showed the Acom aneurysm rupture. Due to above reason, emergency surgery of EVD and ICP monitor was inserted. After surgery, he was sent to SICU for further management.</div></div><div></div>